PLACES=[
# ---- the Upstate ----
N('poinsett-bridge-sc','Poinsett Bridge','Travelers Rest','sc','landmark','standing',35.12947,-82.38428,'exact','public','walk','','1820',
  'A stone bridge built in 1820 on the Old Buncombe Road (the State Road from Charleston towards Saluda Mountain), carrying it over Little Gap Creek with a 14 ft pointed Gothic arch; it is 130 ft long and may have been designed by Robert Mills. It is named for Joel Roberts Poinsett. SCDNR calls it the oldest surviving stone bridge in South Carolina and perhaps in the Southeast. No longer in use; listed on the National Register in 1970.',
  'The dry-laid stone bridge and its pointed arch over the creek, largely intact. Ghost stories have been told about it for decades (Wikipedia). SCDNR says stabilization of the bridge is among its plans, so expect work there.',
  'The oldest bridge in South Carolina, a Gothic arch in the woods off US 25.',
  'Part of Poinsett Bridge Heritage Preserve, owned by SCDNR\'s Heritage Trust Program: 120 acres bought in 2003 to protect the bridge, enlarged to 396 acres in 2024 with new land opened to the public (SCDNR news, June 14, 2024). The same release names improved parking and new trails as future plans. Off US 25 north of Greenville. Preserve hours not checked.',
  [W('Poinsett Bridge'),L('SCDNR: Poinsett Bridge Heritage Preserve addition (2024)','https://dnr.sc.gov/news/2024/Jun/jun14-poinsett.php')],
  conf='',brick='no',th=[35.1289,-82.38418],thn='Lot on Callahan Mountain Road'),
N('campbells-covered-bridge','Campbell\'s Covered Bridge','Gowensville','sc','landmark','standing',35.08583,-82.26417,'exact','public','walk','','1909',
  'The last covered bridge left in South Carolina, built in 1909 by Charles Irwin Willis over Beaverdam Creek and named for Alexander Lafayette Campbell, who ran a grist mill nearby. It is a four-span Howe truss, 38 ft long and 12 ft wide, with iron rods and pine diagonals. Greenville County closed it to cars in the early 1980s; it was restored in 1964 and 1990 and listed on the National Register in 2009.',
  'The timber bridge itself, and portions of Campbell\'s grist mill, which Wikipedia says remain nearby (what is left of the mill not checked).',
  'South Carolina\'s one covered bridge, and the mill it was named for.',
  'Owned by Greenville County; the 10 acres round it were bought by the Greenville County Recreation District in 2008 and made a park (Wikipedia). Off Pleasant Hill Road near Gowensville. The recreation district\'s page would not read, so hours not checked.',
  [W('Campbell\'s Covered Bridge')],
  conf='',brick='no',th=[35.08572,-82.26301],thn='Park lot east of the bridge'),
N('hagood-mill-pickens','Hagood Mill','Pickens','sc','industry','standing',34.92648,-82.72163,'site','open','drive','','1845–1966',
  'A water-powered grist mill built (or rebuilt) in 1845 by James Hagood on a creek where mills had stood since the 1790s. It stayed in the family and ground corn until 1966. Given to the Pickens County Museum in 1973, its wheel and works were rebuilt in the mid-1970s from much of the old fabric and restored again in the 1990s, and it still runs.',
  'An unpainted two-story mill of hand-hewn logs under clapboards, with a 20 ft overshot wooden wheel, an 18 ft ring gear and two granite millstones; the earthen headrace runs from the old dam site 1,650 ft upstream, its last 80 ft in wood. The site also has two old cabins, a blacksmith shop, a still, a cotton gin, the Hagood Creek petroglyphs and the 1930 Prater\'s Creek steel bridge, moved here in 2007 (Wikipedia).',
  'A working 1845 log mill with rock carvings beside it.',
  'Hagood Mill Historic Site & Folklife Center, run under the Pickens County Museum (Wikipedia). Its own site, hagoodmill.org, now shows a domain for sale, and no page of whoever runs it could be read: hours not checked.',
  [W('Hagood Mill')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours='?',th=[34.92601,-82.72237],thn='Lot on Hagood Mill Road'),
N('oconee-station-sc','Oconee Station and the Richards House','Walhalla','sc','fort','standing',34.84611,-83.07056,'site','open','walk','','1792–1799 (blockhouse); 1805 (house)',
  'A stone blockhouse built about 1792 as an outpost of the South Carolina state militia on what was then the western frontier; the troops left in 1799. William Richards built his house beside it in 1805 as a home and trading post. Both are a state historic site, listed on the National Register in 1971.',
  'The stone blockhouse and the Richards House on the hill above the parking lot, with waysides outside. You can see inside when the park interpreter is there (park page). A 1.5-mile trail leads to Station Cove Falls in Sumter National Forest. Interior fittings not checked.',
  'A 1790s frontier blockhouse that is still standing.',
  'Oconee Station State Historic Site, South Carolina State Parks, 500 Oconee Station Road. Free admission. Call (864) 638-0079 for ranger-guided tours (park page, read November 2026).',
  [W('Oconee Station State Historic Site'),P('Oconee Station','oconee-station')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Grounds 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. daily; inside the buildings on ranger tours, by calling (864) 638-0079'),
N('old-stone-church-clemson','Old Stone Church and cemetery','Clemson','sc','worship','standing',34.66444,-82.81528,'exact','view','drive','','1797–1802',
  'Hopewell (Presbyterian) congregation\'s first, log church burned in 1796; John Rusk built this one of field stone and mortar between 1797 and 1802. General Andrew Pickens and Colonel Robert Anderson were early members and are buried in the graveyard. The congregation moved into Pendleton in 1824, and the stone church was used only now and then after that.',
  'The plain field-stone church in its graveyard, between Pendleton and Clemson. Wikipedia says it was built with wooden pews and a pulpit; what is inside now and whether it opens are not checked.',
  'A field-stone church of 1802 with a Revolutionary general in its yard.',
  'In Clemson\'s city limits, between Pendleton and Clemson. Who owns and opens it was not read from a page of its own (not checked); look from the churchyard.',
  [W('Old Stone Church and Cemetery')],
  conf='',brick='no'),
# ---- the Midlands ----
N('star-fort-ninety-six','Star Fort, Ninety Six National Historic Site','Ninety Six','sc','fort','traces',34.14614,-82.01836,'exact','public','walk','','1781',
  'Ninety Six was a village of about 100 settlers by the Revolution. In 1781 a 28-day siege centred on an earthen star fort here. The National Park Service has kept the site of the old town since 1976.',
  'A one-mile interpretive trail from the visitor center reaches the remains of the Star Fort and the original site of Ninety Six; other trails lead to Star Fort Pond, an old unidentified cemetery and the graves of James Gouedy and James Mayson (Wikipedia). The log house opens only on special occasions (NPS).',
  'The earthwork at the heart of a 28-day siege in 1781.',
  'Ninety Six National Historic Site, National Park Service. Visitor center Wednesday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., closed on several federal holidays; the parking lot gate closes at 4:30 p.m. (NPS hours page, read November 2026). Pin is the OpenStreetMap "Star Fort" point; the visitor center lot is the parking.',
  [W('Ninety Six National Historic Site'),L('NPS: Ninety Six, hours','https://www.nps.gov/nisi/planyourvisit/hours.htm')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours='Grounds dawn to dusk daily; visitor center Wednesday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.',th=[34.14663,-82.02334],thn='Visitor center, 1103 Highway 248 S'),
N('columbia-canal-riverfront','Columbia Canal (Riverfront Park)','Columbia','sc','canal','traces',34.02083,-81.06194,'area','public','walk','canal','1824; rebuilt 1891',
  'Built by the state and finished in 1824 to carry boats past the falls where the Broad and Saluda join to form the Congaree, with four lift locks and a guard lock; the work was done by Irish laborers. Railroads took its trade after 1842. In 1888–1891 it was rebuilt as a power canal 150 ft wide, feeding the Columbia Mill (now the State Museum) and a hydro plant at its south end.',
  'A three-mile trail on the levee between the canal and the Broad River. Wikipedia says parts of the 1824 canal south of Gervais Street and of the Bull Sluice canal survive; the City says Granby Park, downstream, keeps a preserved section of the 1824 canal and a brick cistern of the State Distillery (not checked on the ground).',
  'The canal that powered the first textile mill run on AC motors.',
  'Riverfront Park, City of Columbia Parks and Recreation. Much of the park is being renovated; the southern part is open from 312 Laurel Street (City parks page, read November 2026). Granby Park is open dawn to dusk. The park\'s own hours not checked.',
  [W('Columbia Canal'),L('City of Columbia: regional parks','https://parksandrec.columbiasc.gov/regionalparks/')],
  conf='',brick='?'),
N('historic-camden-sc','Historic Camden Revolutionary War Site','Camden','sc','town','standing',34.23389,-80.60333,'site','open','walk','','1780–1781 (British post); houses about 1785–1830',
  'Camden was the main British supply post in the back country from the spring of 1780 to the spring of 1781, a town of two blocks inside a palisade with five redoubts. The 107-acre site keeps a mix of moved, restored and reconstructed buildings: the Kershaw-Cornwallis House is a 1977 reconstruction of the British headquarters.',
  'The 1785 John Craven House (restored and furnished), the 1830 Cunningham House, two log houses of about 1800 with exhibits, the rebuilt moat and reconstructed fortifications (Wikipedia). Visitors may walk through the houses on a self-guided pass (Historic Camden).',
  'A Revolutionary town where most of what you see was rebuilt.',
  'Run by Historic Camden, 222 Broad Street. The grounds can be walked any time in the day for free; passes and tours are sold at the gift shops; battlefield tours usually at 2 p.m. Wednesday to Saturday, call ahead (admission page, read November 2026).',
  [W('Historic Camden Revolutionary War Site'),L('Historic Camden: admission','https://www.historiccamden.org/admission')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Self-guided passes Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; guided tours 11:30 a.m. and 2:30 p.m.'),
N('rose-hill-plantation-union','Rose Hill (Gist mansion)','Union','sc','landmark','standing',34.60533,-81.66574,'site','open','drive','','about 1811–1830; remodelled late 1850s',
  'A Georgian brick house built about 1811–1830 for the Gist family and remodelled by Governor William H. Gist in the late 1850s, who stuccoed it and added two-tier porches. It served as the governor\'s mansion in 1858–1860, and from here Gist wrote to other Southern governors about seceding. The Forest Service bought it in 1938; a collector restored it from 1942 and sold it to the state park service in 1960.',
  'The stuccoed three-story house with its spiral stair and the second-floor ballroom; a brick kitchen of the 1850s, the only other building from before the war; gardens, and a 1.25-mile trail to the Tyger River (Wikipedia). Inside on a guided tour only.',
  'The house where secession was plotted, kept as it was.',
  'Rose Hill Plantation State Historic Site, South Carolina State Parks. Grounds free; house tours $10 adults (park page, read November 2026).',
  [W('Rose Hill Plantation State Historic Site'),P('Rose Hill Plantation','rose-hill')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Grounds 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. daily; house by guided tour Thursday to Monday at 11 a.m., 1 p.m. and 3 p.m.'),
N('redcliffe-plantation','Redcliffe','Beech Island','sc','landmark','standing',33.41833,-81.87083,'site','open','drive','','1857–1859',
  'A Greek Revival house designed by Louis Berckmans and built in 1857–1859 for James Henry Hammond, home to three generations of his descendants; enslaved families, among them the Henleys, Goodwins and Wigfalls, worked the place. His great-grandson John Shaw Billings gave the estate and its collections to the state in 1973.',
  'The house and the other historic structures, shown on guided tours (park page). What original fabric survives inside not checked.',
  'A planter\'s house given to the state with everything in it.',
  'Redcliffe Plantation State Historic Site, South Carolina State Parks. Park admission free; house tours $10 adults; tours are on a limited schedule, call 803-827-1473 to reserve (park page, read November 2026).',
  [W('Redcliffe Plantation State Historic Site'),P('Redcliffe Plantation','redcliffe')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Grounds 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. daily; house tours on a limited schedule, by reservation (803-827-1473)'),
# ---- the Pee Dee and the coast ----
N('st-davids-cheraw','Old St. David\'s Church','Cheraw','sc','worship','standing',34.695556,-79.879444,'site','view','walk','','1770–1773',
  'The church of St. David\'s Parish, set up by the General Assembly in 1768 and named for the patron saint of Wales, where many Cheraw settlers came from. The frame church was built in 1770–1773, used as a hospital in the Revolution and again in the Civil War, and given a vestibule and a square steeple with diminishing stages early in the 1800s. It is one of the state\'s few church buildings older than the Revolution.',
  'The white frame church, 53 by 30 ft, with its tiered steeple of louvered stages, wooden parapets and corner pinnacles, and the cemetery round it. The 1971 nomination describes two rows of paneled box pews with hinged doors and a west gallery, though the pews were altered in the early 1800s. Interior today not checked.',
  'A 1770s frame church that was a hospital in two wars.',
  'In Cheraw; the regional tourism district invites visitors to walk the cemetery and grounds. Who owns and opens the church was not read from a page of its own: hours not checked.',
  [W('St. David\'s Episcopal Church and Cemetery'),L('National Register nomination: St. David\'s Church','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000761_text'),
   L('Olde English District: visit Old St. David\'s','https://www.oldeenglishdistrict.com/blog/visit-old-st-davids-church/')],
  conf='',brick='no'),
N('prince-george-winyah-church','Prince George Winyah Church','Georgetown','sc','worship','standing',33.36889,-79.28083,'exact','open','walk','','1745–1747; tower 1824',
  'Begun with a cornerstone in 1745 and first used in 1747, this is one of the oldest churches in continuous use in South Carolina. British troops occupied it in the Revolution and burned the interior. A chancel and gallery came about 1809 and the brick tower in 1824. The congregation joined the Anglican Church in North America in 2012.',
  'Built of English red brick with oyster-shell mortar: a five-bay nave with a Dutch gable, a plastered barrel-vaulted ceiling, paneled wooden box pews and a flagstone floor; a stained glass window brought from St. Mary\'s Chapel at Hagley; the churchyard inside a brick wall, with the graves of Paul Trapier and Governor R. F. W. Allston (Wikipedia).',
  'Box pews and a brick wall round the graves, in use since 1747.',
  'Prince George Winyah Parish (Anglican), Georgetown. The parish invites visitors to Sunday worship; its home page listed a 7:45 a.m. Sunday service (read November 2026), and its pages on service times and docents would not open, so other times not checked.',
  [W('Prince George Winyah Parish Church'),L('Prince George Winyah Parish','https://www.pgwinyah.org/')],
  conf='',brick='yes',hours='Sunday worship; a 7:45 a.m. Sunday service was listed (other service times not read)'),
N('atalaya-castle','Atalaya','Murrells Inlet','sc','landmark','standing',33.50167,-79.0675,'exact','open','walk','','1931–1933',
  'The winter home of Archer M. Huntington and the sculptor Anna Hyatt Huntington, built in 1931–1933 by local men in the Depression, apparently without drawn plans, in a Moorish style. The Army Air Corps used it in 1942–1946 and the Huntingtons last wintered here in 1947. The land became Huntington Beach State Park in 1960.',
  'A 200 ft square masonry house round an open court, with a square tower nearly 40 ft high that held a water tank, 30 rooms round three sides, Anna Huntington\'s skylit studio and the pens for her animal models, and iron window grilles she designed (Wikipedia). The furnishings went to New York in the 1950s, so the rooms are bare.',
  'A sculptor\'s Moorish winter castle on the beach.',
  'Huntington Beach State Park, South Carolina State Parks. Park admission $8 adults, Atalaya $2 more; Atalaya may close for events (park page, read November 2026). Parking can fill on weekends and holidays.',
  [W('Atalaya Castle (US)'),P('Huntington Beach','huntington-beach')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='9 a.m. to 4 p.m. daily (to 5 p.m. in Daylight Saving Time); closed for some events'),
N('hampton-plantation-sc','Hampton Plantation house','McClellanville','sc','landmark','standing',33.20118,-79.43455,'exact','open','walk','','1735–1791',
  'A rice plantation worked by hundreds of enslaved people. The house was begun in 1735 by Noe Serre and enlarged by Daniel Horry, who added a ballroom and a bedroom wing; the portico of eight Doric columns, added in 1790–91 in time for George Washington\'s visit, is thought the first temple front on an American house. Its last private owner was the poet Archibald Rutledge; the state bought it in 1971.',
  'The two-and-a-half-story wood house with a hip roof, false shuttered windows on the wings, the columned portico with a carved Adam frieze, and the raised brick foundation; the kitchen stands nearby. Inside only on a guided tour.',
  'The first temple-front house in America, built for Washington\'s visit.',
  'Hampton Plantation State Historic Site, South Carolina State Parks, off US 17 north of McClellanville. Grounds free; house tours $10 adults (park page, read November 2026).',
  [W('Hampton Plantation'),P('Hampton Plantation','hampton')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Grounds 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. daily; mansion by guided tour Friday to Tuesday at noon and 2 p.m.'),
N('pompion-hill-chapel','Pompion Hill Chapel','Huger','sc','worship','standing',33.0863,-79.84027,'exact','view','drive','','1763–1765',
  'A small brick Georgian chapel ("punkin hill") built in 1763–65 by the mason William Axson, with bricks from nearby Parnassus Plantation, on a site above the Cooper River where a wooden church of St. Thomas\' Parish stood before it. It is little changed and was made a National Historic Landmark in 1970.',
  'Flemish-bond brick walls under the original slate roof, arched windows with fanlights and a Palladian window in the chancel; inside, a herringbone brick floor with red tile aisles, a coved plaster ceiling and the original pews, pulpit and woodwork (Wikipedia). Whether it opens was not found.',
  'A 1760s country chapel with its original pews and pulpit.',
  'Rural, a few miles south-west of Huger. Who owns it and whether visitors may go in was not found on any page read: look from the way in; hours not checked.',
  [W('Pompion Hill Chapel')],
  conf='',brick='?'),
N('biggin-church-ruins','Biggin Church ruins','Moncks Corner','sc','worship','ruins',33.2125,-79.96694,'site','view','drive','','1761 (ruins); first church about 1711',
  'The church of St. John\'s, Berkeley, the largest of the colony\'s first parishes. A first brick church of about 1711 burned in a forest fire about 1755; its 1761 successor was a British depot in the Revolution and was burned as they retreated in 1781, then rebuilt. Damaged in the Civil War and neglected, it burned again in a forest fire around 1886 or in the 1890s, and its bricks were carried off.',
  'Two brick walls of a church about 30 by 60 ft: one with a large doorway in a Gibbs surround between pairs of arched windows, the other a gable end with a door and windows, quoins at the corner and a rounded-brick water table. A cemetery beside it is still used, with the grave of Sir John Colleton III (Wikipedia).',
  'Two walls left of a church burned three times.',
  'About 2 miles from Moncks Corner near SC 402. Wikipedia says the ruins remain part of Strawberry Chapel\'s land (2019). No page of the owners was read: look from the road edge; hours not checked.',
  [W('Biggin Church Ruins')],
  conf='',brick='?'),
N('old-santee-canal-park','Old Santee Canal Park','Moncks Corner','sc','canal','traces',33.19322,-79.97274,'area','public','walk','canal','1793–1800 (canal); 1843 (house)',
  'The Santee Canal, 22 miles long with ten locks, was built in 1793–1800 under John Christian Senf to link the Cooper River with the Santee and so Charleston with Columbia. It never paid, lost its charter in 1853 and was out of use after 1865; much of it lies under Lake Moultrie. The 195-acre park, opened in 1989, is at Stony Landing, the canal\'s southern end.',
  'The 1843 Stony Landing House, an interpretive center, and four miles of boardwalks along Biggin Creek and its swamp (park page). Which canal works can be seen in the park is not said on the pages read: not checked.',
  'The southern end of one of America\'s first canals.',
  'Old Santee Canal Park, 900 Stony Landing Road; the pages read do not say who owns the park (not checked). Admission $5 a person (park page, read November 2026). Pin is the park address, not a canal feature.',
  [W('Santee Canal'),L('Old Santee Canal Park','https://oldsanteecanalpark.org/')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='9 a.m. to 5 p.m. daily'),
N('colonial-dorchester-sc','Colonial Dorchester: tabby fort and St. George\'s bell tower','Summerville','sc','town','ruins',32.9495,-80.1719,'site','public','walk','','1696–1780s',
  'A town founded in 1696 by Congregationalists from Dorchester, Massachusetts, on the Ashley River; a trading centre of about 40 houses by 1781. A tabby fort and powder magazine were built in 1757–1760. The British burned St. George\'s church in 1781, and the town was abandoned; scavengers took its bricks but left the 1751 bell tower, which the 1886 earthquake split and which was repaired in the early 1960s.',
  'The walls of the oyster-shell tabby fort, called one of the best kept in the country, the church bell tower, the sites of wharves and graves, and archaeology still going on (Wikipedia).',
  'A vanished town with its tabby fort and a lone bell tower.',
  'Colonial Dorchester State Historic Site, South Carolina State Parks. Admission $3 adults (park page, read November 2026).',
  [W('Colonial Dorchester State Historic Site'),P('Colonial Dorchester','colonial-dorchester')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='9 a.m. to 6 p.m. daily in Daylight Saving Time; 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. the rest of the year'),
N('fort-moultrie-sc','Fort Moultrie','Sullivan\'s Island','sc','fort','standing',32.75939,-79.85779,'exact','open','walk','forts','1808–1809 (present fort); 1776 (first fort)',
  'The first fort here, of palmetto logs and still unfinished in June 1776, was held by William Moultrie\'s men in the Battle of Sullivan\'s Island and inspired the state\'s flag and nickname. A storm destroyed a later fort in 1804, and the present brick fort was built in 1808–09 under Alexander Macomb. It was rearmed with new batteries (Jasper, Gadsden and others) until the Army lowered its flag for the last time in 1947; the National Park Service took it in 1960.',
  'The brick fort of 1808–09 and the later batteries round it; the former mine storehouse is now park offices (Wikipedia). Interior fabric not checked.',
  'The fort whose palmetto logs gave South Carolina its flag.',
  'Fort Sumter and Fort Moultrie National Historical Park, National Park Service. A $10 site fee per adult; closed New Year\'s Day, Thanksgiving and Christmas (NPS pages, read November 2026).',
  [W('Fort Moultrie'),L('NPS: Fort Sumter and Fort Moultrie, hours','https://www.nps.gov/fosu/planyourvisit/hours.htm'),L('NPS: fees','https://www.nps.gov/fosu/planyourvisit/fees.htm')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. daily'),
]

